= Public Blog: Why the Label Lies

#no-indent[
Stand under a misting nozzle and the brochure feels true. Sit three metres away on a humid afternoon and the street returns. Labels quote the nozzle; shelters live at the bench.

Our break-even humidity sits near fifty-four percent once pad efficiency is allowed to fall in moist air—about sixty-four percent if that efficiency is frozen. Below the line, evaporative cooling can earn its water. Above it, a plain fan moves sweat more honestly per kilowatt. Bigger pads do not automatically win outdoors: a stronger jet can throw cold air into the pavement and show a *lower* seat delivery fraction.

One more honesty check: a machine can look efficient and still be unsafe or unaffordable. On a very hot dry day every option we tested can leave people above a 35 °C felt line. On a tight shelter budget the fan is often the only purchase that clears the books. Cooling that reaches the person is the only cooling that counts.
]
